  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Cold War Triangle shows early scholarly uptake alongside modest but real readership: OpenAlex records 2 citations, both appearing in 2021, and OAPEN reports 20 downloads. The citation trail already reaches across biomedical and history-of-science venues, suggesting the book is being noticed by readers interested in antiviral research as well as the Cold War’s scientific networks.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Renilde Loeckx’s Cold War Triangle, published by Leuven University Press in 2021, is positioned at the intersection of Cold War history, HIV/AIDS, virology, and the history of medicine. The record shows an open-access book with early but visible scholarly use: 2 citations are indexed in OpenAlex, and both appeared in the publication year. That is a small citation base, but it is nonetheless a concrete sign that the book has begun to enter academic conversation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citing works point to a cross-disciplinary reception. One citation appears in Biochemical Pharmacology in a piece on antiviral research and drug development, while the other appears in Isis in a current bibliography of the history of science. This mix suggests the book is being read both as part of biomedical discussions around antivirals and as a contribution to the historiography of science. In other words, its influence is not confined to a single field.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data adds a separate, complementary signal. OAPEN reports 20 downloads, indicating that readers have accessed the book directly through an open-access distribution channel. For a scholarly book, that level of early access is modest but meaningful, especially when considered alongside the citation record and the book’s open availability through repository infrastructure.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The surrounding record reinforces that this is an openly shared work with emerging visibility rather than a long-established citation anchor. There is no event archive available here, and no broader public-discourse signal is captured in the dataset. Taken together, the evidence points to a book that has started to circulate in scholarly and reader communities, with its strongest signals coming from early citations, open access distribution, and interdisciplinary relevance.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
